port documents LMEvalJob's current capabilities and\n  limitations as an execution engine for standard and custom lm-eval tasks,\n  with evidence from the operator source and CRD definitions.\n- The report documents the Llama Stack eval provider API contract and states\n  explicitly whether LMEvalJob can back it directly, can be extended to back\n  it, or cannot back it.\n- A single recommendation (Outcome A or Outcome B) is made with decision\n  criteria, covering impact on the execution flow, result persistence capture\n  point, and the Llama Stack provider integration.\n- The recommendation identifies which downstream work changes under the\n  non-chosen outcome, so downstream plans can be adjusted without re-analysis.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Gates HLR-1 (P0), and transitively HLR-2, HLR-3, HLR-4, HLR-5, HLR-6, HLR-7:\n  the execution engine decision determines what the evaluation launch\n  capability, suite delivery, result capture, Dashboard surface, pipeline step,\n  custom task flow, and Llama Stack provider all build against.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":38599,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a39b20e2ca4c4ad7","agent_id":"a3d693597663ee266","session_id":"811fcb9e-f25c-43e8-a4d0-4711eb32341e","uuid":"9510fb5f-5432-4c25-99cf-68c32b770944","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:26:16.516Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9002"}
