ack | RHOAI GenAI | P1 |\n34\t| E008 | Dashboard views for evaluation runs and model comparison | Implementation | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n35\t| E009 | Evaluation step for Data Science Pipelines | Implementation | Data Science Pipelines | RHOAI Pipelines | P1 |\n36\t| E010 | Custom evaluation task support | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n37\t| E011 | End-to-end product documentation for the evaluation workflow | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n38\t\n39\tMain-plan epics: E001–E003, E005–E011. E004 exists only as conditional branch\n40\tepics (see Conditional Branches below), selected by the outcome of E001.\n41\t\n42\tPriorities: E001–E003 gate P0 work and therefore inherit P0. E011 is a\n43\t`docs-authoring` epic exempt from priority inheritance; per the docs-authoring\n44\tpriority exception its priority derives from the strategy frontmatter\n45\t(`priority: Critical`) → P0, even though HLR-9 is P2 (see Health Warnings).\n46\t\n47\t## Dependency DAG\n48\t\n49\t```mermaid\n50\tgraph TD\n51\t  E001[\"E001 Investigation: execution engine\"]\n52\t  E002[\"E002 Investigation: result schema\"]\n53\t  E003[\"E003 Investigation: benchmark licensing\"]\n54\t  BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004: extend LMEvalJob (branch A)\"]\n55\t  BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004: new evaluation service (branch B)\"]\n56\t  E005[\"E005 Persist results to Model Registry\"]\n57\t  E006[\"E006 Standard benchmark suites\"]\n58\t  E007[\"E007 Llama Stack eval provider\"]\n59\t  E008[\"E008 Dashboard evaluation views\"]\n60\t  E009[\"E009 Pipelines evaluation step\"]\n61\t  E010[\"E010 Custom evaluation tasks\"]\n62\t  E011[\"E011 Documentation\"]\n63\t\n64\t  E001 --> BA\n65\t  E001 --> BB\n66\t  E001 --> E005\n67\t  E001 --> E006\n68\t  E001 --> E007\n69\t  E001 --> E008\n70\t  E001 --> E009\n71\t  E001 --> E010\n72\t  E002 --> E005\n73\t  E003 --> E006\n74\t  E005 --> E008\n75\t  E005 --> E011\n76\t  E006 --> E011\n77\t  E007 --> E011\n78\t  E008 --> E011\n79\t  E009 --> E011\n80\t  E010 --> E011\n81\t```\n82\t\n83\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (\"E001 --> E005\" = E001 must complete\n84\tbefore E005 starts). Branch epics BA/BB are alternative instantiations of the\n85\tE004 slot, not parallel work.\n86\t\n87\t## DAG Justification\n88\t\n89\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n90\t|------|------|-----------|\n91\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004 | Rule 3 | Engine decision selects the branch; branch epic exists only under outcome A. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n92\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 | Engine decision selects the branch; branch epic exists only under outcome B. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n93\t| E001 → E005 | Rules 3, 13 | The chosen engine determines the interface evaluation results are captured from for persistence; approach changes with the outcome (`rewrite`). |\n94\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 3 | Suite packaging and invocation are defined by the execution engine; scope changes with the outcome (`rewrite`). |\n95\t| E001 → E007 | Rules 3, 12 | The Llama Stack eval provider adapter builds against the platform evaluation service, whose form is decided by the investigation (`rewrite`). Main-plan epic (exists under both outcomes), so it depends on the Investigation, not on a branch epic. |\n96\t| E001 → E008 | Rules 3, 13 | The Dashboard surfaces run status via the engine-determined CR/API surface (`rewrite`). |\n97\t| E001 → E009 | Rules 3, 12 | The pipeline step wraps the engine-determined evaluation launch interface (`rewrite`). |\n98\t| E001 → E010 | Rules 3, 12 | Custom tasks run through the same execution flow chosen by the investigation (`rewrite`). |\n99\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 | The canonical result schema is a true gate: persistence scope is rewritten around the prototyped schema (`rewrite`). |\n100\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | Licensing outcome rewrites suite delivery (bundled redistribution vs customer-fetch mechanism) (`rewrite`). |\n101\t| E005 → E008 | Rule 13 | Side-by-side comparison of two model versions builds against persisted, version-linked results produced by the persistence work. |\n102\t| E005 → E011 | Rule 11 | Docs are blocked by all Implementation epics; always last. |\n103\t| E006 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n104\t| E007 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n105\t| E008 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n106\t| E009 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n107\t| E010 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n108\t\n109\tDeliberately absent edges:\n110\t\n111\t- No `konflux-onboarding` edges: every affected component is already Konflux\n112\t  onboarded per architecture context, so no onboarding chain is generated\n113\t  (Rules 19–20 do not fire — no new component enters the platform).\n114\t- E007/E008/E009/E010 do **not** depend on the branch E004 epics: branch epics\n115\t  are not main-plan nodes, and these epics exist under both outcomes. They\n116\t  depend on the outcome of the Investigation (E001) instead.\n117\t- E005 and E006 are parallel (different components/teams, no shared artifact).\n118\t- E007, E008, E009, E010 are mutually parallel (different components/teams).\n119\t\n120\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n121\t\n122\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n123\t|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n124\t| HLR-1 | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | Evaluation launch against served models (KServe endpoint or Llama Stack distribution) — exactly one branch epic is instantiated. |\n125\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E006 | Standard suites (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag via lm-eval task names) out of the box. |\n126\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E005 | Results persisted and linked to the Model Registry version. |\n127\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E010 | Custom evaluation tasks (own datasets/metrics) through the same flow. |\n128\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E008 | Dashboard runs/status/scores and side-by-side version comparison. |\n129\t| HLR-6 | P1 | E009 | Evaluation as a Data Science Pipelines step for promotion gating. |\n130\t| HLR-7 | P1 | E007 | Llama Stack eval provider API backed by the platform service. |\n131\t| HLR-8 | P2 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004, E005 | Cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) is recorded by the execution capability and persisted with results. Bundled, not split: it shares the run-record data structure and persistence surface with the P0 work — splitting would duplicate the same schema (Step 4 priority-split test fails). |\n132\t| HLR-9 | P2 | E011 | End-to-end product documentation including custom task authoring. |\n133\t\n134\tAll nine HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n135\t\n136\t## Open Question Disposition\n137\t\n138\t| Open question | Disposition | Rationale |\n139\t|---------------|-------------|-----------|\n140\t| 1. Execution engine (LMEvalJob vs new service) | Investigation epic E001 with conditional branches | The strategy explicitly requests a technical investigation with a recommendation; the answer changes which execution epic exists and what every integration epic builds against. Two bounded outcomes → branch epics for the E004 slot. |\n141\t| 2. Result schema for Model Registry custom properties | Investigation epic E002 | Strategy requires a prototype against real MMLU output before committing; the answer rewrites the persistence epic (E005). Unbounded schema design → gate, no branches. |\n142\t| 3. Benchmark dataset licensing / redistribution | Investigation epic E003 | The answer rewrites the out-of-the-box suite delivery epic (E006): bundled redistribution vs customer-fetch mechanism. |\n143\t\n144\t## Conditional Branches\n145\t\n146\tThe execution-engine investigation (E001) has exactly two bounded outcomes:\n147\t\n148\t- **Branch A** — build on TrustyAI's LMEvalJob (Tech Preview) as the single\n149\t  execution path: `RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E004.md`.\n150\t- **Branch B** — a new evaluation service that also serves Llama Stack's eval\n151\t  provider API: `RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004.md`.\n152\t\n153\tBoth branch files set `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9002-E001`, include E001 in\n154\t`dependencies`, and carry `gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic lists a\n155\tbranch epic in its `dependencies`; epics that depend on the execution\n156\tcapability depend on E001 (the Investigation) instead. Branch epics are\n157\texcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n158\t\n159\t## Component Mapping Notes\n160\t\n161\t- All strategy components map 1:1 to canonical names in\n162\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt` (TrustyAI, Llama Stack, Dashboard, Data\n163\t  Science Pipelines, Model Registry).\n164\t- All five have architecture context files → all are in the platform; no\n165\t  repo-onboarding / konflux-onboarding / license-validation chain is generated.\n166\t- BRANCH-B-E004 (a new evaluation service) has no canonical component of its\n167\t  own; it is filed under the closest parent-level component **TrustyAI** (the\n168\t  platform's existing model-quality/evaluation component). If branch B is\n169\t  selected, a component rename/registration may be requested at execution time.\n170\t- Documentation is a canonical component but has no architecture context file;\n171\t  it is a content (not code) component, handled as `docs-authoring` with no\n172\t  provisioning chain.\n173\t- **Model Registry is treated as active**, not passive: while custom properties\n174\t  already exist as the extension point, the strategy's linkage requirement\n175\t  (results tied to the *deployed* model version) builds on the registry's\n176\t  role as source of truth linking model versions to serving deployments, so\n177\t  E002/E005 are owned by the Model Registry team.\n178\t\n179\t## Health Warnings\n180\t\n181\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n182\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n183\t  no Non-Functional Requirements, no Technical Approach, and no Staff Engineer\n184\t  Input. Teams were derived from architecture-context owning teams; the\n185\t  Documentation epic's team (\"RHOAI Documentation\") is assumed since no owner\n186\t  is stated anywhere. Acceptance criteria below are derived from HLRs.\n187\t- **Docs priority inversion (rule-driven)**: HLR-9 is P2, but the docs-authoring\n188\t  priority exception derives the epic's priority from the strategy-level\n189\t  `priority: Critical` → P0. Flagged so a human can confirm this is intended.\n190\t- **Scope trap — disconnected environments**: HLR-2 promises out-of-the-box\n191\t  suites, but redistribution in disconnected environments is license-gated\n192\t  (E003). If most suites turn out to be customer-fetch, HLR-2's \"out of the\n193\t  box\" promise degrades; the E003 gate exists to surface this before\n194\t  implementation.\n195\t- **Tech Preview foundation**: Branch A builds on the LMEvalJob integration,\n196\t  which is Tech Preview; graduation risk is inherent to that outcome.\n197\t\n198\t## Tiered Delivery\n199\t\n200\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing: all\n201\tcomponents are in-platform, and the upstream dependencies (lm-evaluation-harness,\n202\tLlama Stack) are already integrated. The conditional branches above handle the\n203\tstrategy's internal uncertainty instead.\n204\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb142f201910a446","agent_id":"a7feea8f854dd58c8","session_id":"811fcb9e-f25c-43e8-a4d0-4711eb32341e","uuid":"b641777a-5083-4d17-b340-204d94672027","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:33:58.357Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae54aba5ad63e866","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
