{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-153da89d-e653-473c-9c50-289c94a8aac3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9604cb5b5788fa48","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-k3s_reif/artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-9002-decomp-review.md","content":"# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9002\n\n## Review Summary\n\nScore: 12/14 — pass\nRecommendation: accept\n\nFull decomposition (triage: null, 12 epics including 2 conditional branch epics).\nStructurally sound: DAG diagram and epic frontmatter dependencies match exactly,\nbranch files are correctly configured, HLR coverage is complete, and all\narchitecture claims verify against `.context/architecture-context/`. Two\ncriteria score 1 for metadata-level defects (incomplete `gated_by` coverage on\nepics with two Investigation dependencies; inconsistent `human_process_gates`\nsignal values across epics with identically-worded rationales). Both fall\nwithin those criteria's explicit 1-point bands (\"gating metadata has issues\";\n\"1-2 arguable signal values\"), so they score 1 rather than 0; they are recorded\nas issues for a future revision pass but do not warrant blocking the\ndecomposition.\n\n## Criterion Details\n\n### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n\nAll nine HLRs map to at least one epic; the traceability matrix in the summary\nis present and accurate against the epic files' HLR Traceability sections.\nAll P0 HLRs (HLR-1 via the branch E004 epics, HLR-2 via E006, HLR-3 via E005)\nare covered by P0 epics. E001–E003 correctly inherit P0 (they gate P0 work).\nE007–E010 are P1, matching their P1 HLRs. E011 (docs-authoring) is P0 via the\ndocs-authoring priority exception (strategy `priority: Critical` → P0), which\nis correctly applied and flagged in Health Warnings. HLR-8 (P2) is bundled\ninto the P0 execution/persistence epics with a valid justification — it shares\nthe run-record data structure and persistence surface with the P0 work, so it\nis coupled scope rather than a deferrable feature (no priority collapse). HLR-9\n(P2) is covered by E011. No orphaned requirements.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nNo circular dependencies. Every edge in the summary's Mermaid diagram (drawn\ndependency → dependent, per convention) has a matching `dependencies` entry in\nthe target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa — verified edge by edge for all\n12 epics. No konflux-onboarding / license-validation / repo-onboarding epics\nexist, and correctly so: all five affected components have architecture\ncontext files, so no onboarding chain is generated. Branch files\nBRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004 both set `branch` (A/B, matching filename) and\n`gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9002-E001` with E001 in `dependencies`; no main-plan epic\nlists a branch epic in its `dependencies` (E007–E010 correctly depend on the\nInvestigation E001 instead, as required). Rule 11 edges (all implementations →\nE011) are present and correctly do not trigger priority inheritance. Critical\npath length 4 (E001 → E005 → E008 → E011) is reasonable for a strategy of this\nsize. E005/E006 and E007/E008/E009/E010 parallelism is preserved.\n\nMinor: E007's and E010's acceptance criteria assert outcomes that depend on\nE005's and E008's artifacts (\"results are persisted against the evaluated\nmodel's registered version\"; custom task results \"appear in the same result\nviews/comparisons\") without DAG edges to those epics. This is defensible —\nboth epics delegate to the execution capability rather than building against\nthe registry API directly (unlike E008's comparison view, which correctly has\nan E005 edge), so these read as integration-level ACs — but the DAG does not\nguarantee E005/E008 land before E007/E010's ACs can be validated.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has a single component/team tuple, and all team assignments match\nthe owning teams in `.context/architecture-context/` (TrustyAI/TrustyAI,\nModel Registry/Model Registry, Llama Stack/RHOAI GenAI, Dashboard/RHOAI\nDashboard, Data Science Pipelines/RHOAI Pipelines, Documentation/RHOAI\nDocumentation). No epic bundles multiple components or teams. No scope\nduplication across siblings: the branch E004 epics build the execution\ncapability, E006 builds the suite catalog/delivery, E008 is the only UI epic,\nE005 the only persistence epic. No work-product type mixing — E011 is the sole\ncontent epic (docs-authoring); E009's step usage material is incidental\ndocumentation, correctly folded into the code epic.\n\nMinor: BRANCH-B-E004 (greenfield service: execution API, run lifecycle,\nproject-scoped multi-tenancy, lm-eval orchestration, provider adapter surface,\nper-project deployment) is at risk of exceeding ~2 weeks. Acceptable as a\nsingle branch instantiation mirroring branch A's functional envelope, but if\nE001 selects outcome B and the recommended architecture is large, it should be\nsplit at instantiation time.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n\nTypes are correct: E001–E003 are genuine Investigations (each resolves a\nstrategy open question whose answer rewrites downstream epics — engine choice,\nresult schema, licensing), and every Implementation epic produces a concrete\nartifact. All `gated_by` targets appear in the corresponding epic's direct\n`dependencies`. All epics with `gated_by` have `gate_failure_impact` with both\n`action` and `fallback_approach` populated.\n\nTwo gating metadata defects (within this criterion's 1-point band):\n\n- E005 depends on two Investigations (E001, E002) but the scalar `gated_by`\n  records only E002; E006 depends on E001 and E003 but `gated_by` records only\n  E003. The decomposition's own DAG justification labels E001→E005 and E001→E006\n  as rewrite gates (\"approach/scope changes with the outcome\"), so the\n  engine-decision gate on the persistence and suite-delivery epics is not\n  recorded in `gated_by` — automated consumers that detect gates from\n  `gated_by` will miss it. (The schema permits only one `gated_by` value; the\n  summary or justification should at minimum reconcile this, e.g. by\n  justifying E001→E005/E006 as Rule 13 build-against edges rather than gates.)\n- BRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004 set `gate_failure_impact.action: rewrite`,\n  but their `fallback_approach` text says the epic \"is removed\" when the other\n  outcome is selected. Rule 3 maps \"epic may not exist\" to `remove`; the action\n  value contradicts the stated fallback.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n\nCorrect rubric usage throughout: all three Investigation epics carry\n`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric), all Implementation epics (including\nboth branch epics and the docs-authoring epic) carry `ai_signals` (9-signal\nrubric), and every epic has an ai-signals rationale file whose values match the\nfrontmatter. Investigation signal values are well-justified against the rubric\n(e.g. E001 `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` for peripheral cluster\nvalidation; E003 `human_judgment_required: -1` for peripheral legal review).\nComputed `ai_implementability` fields were not checked, per instructions.\n\nOne inconsistency (conservative-direction, does not change any routing\noutcome): `human_process_gates` is scored 0 on E007 and BRANCH-B-E004 with\nrationales (\"No human approval step beyond standard review\" / \"No stated human\napproval gate; standard review applies\") that are treated as +1 (\"None\") on\nE005, E009, and E010 with identically-worded rationales. Per the rubric's +1\ncondition (\"None\"), E007 and BRANCH-B-E004 should be +1. E008 and\nBRANCH-A-E004 (0, with hedged rationales mentioning possible UX sign-off /\nTech Preview release scrutiny) are arguable but defensible.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has concrete, testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated\nACs are present wherever applicable: BRANCH-A-E004 has a rollback/non-regression\nAC for the extended Tech Preview path; E008 has a feature-flag AC (off by\ndefault, via the Dashboard's standard per-component mechanism); E011\n(docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC; E005 has a\nregistry-health AC; implementation epics carry build-pipeline-green ACs. No\nepic body references sibling epics by draft ID — verified by grep; dependency\nrelationships live only in frontmatter, and bodies describe dependencies in\nplain terms (\"the result schema prototype\", \"the evaluation execution\ncapability\").\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is covered: all nine HLRs, all three open questions\ndispositioned to Investigation epics E001/E002/E003 with bounded outcomes\n(two branches) for the engine question, out-of-scope commitments respected\n(E009 explicitly keeps humans in the promotion decision; no arena-style or\nfine-tuning feedback work appears anywhere), and strategy-level context\ncarried forward (Tech Preview graduation risk → BRANCH-A-E004 scope and Health\nWarnings; disconnected-environment licensing risk → E003/E006 and E011 scope).\nCross-epic consistency holds: both evaluation targets (KServe endpoint, Llama\nStack distribution) named in HLR-1 appear in the branch epics' scope and ACs;\nthe schema work in E002 covers scores, suite versions, and cost/duration\nmetadata, all of which E005 persists. Documentation support is covered by the\nE011 docs-authoring epic (Rule 11). Missing strategy sections (Impacted Teams,\nACs, NFRs) are explicitly flagged as health warnings rather than silently\ndropped.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":94950,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb142f201910a446","agent_id":"a7feea8f854dd58c8","session_id":"811fcb9e-f25c-43e8-a4d0-4711eb32341e","uuid":"be6306ba-913e-48b0-b975-236be716aae6","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:56:07.007Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RH