{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89eccbb91b9aa244","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9002\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n9\t  description: 'E005 and E006 each depend on two Investigation epics (E005: E001+E002;\n10\t    E006: E001+E003) but the scalar gated_by records only E002 and E003. The DAG justification\n11\t    itself labels E001→E005 and E001→E006 as rewrite gates, so the engine-decision\n12\t    gate on the persistence and suite-delivery epics is unrecorded in gated_by; gate-detecting\n13\t    consumers will miss it. Reconcile by recording the gate or re-justifying those\n14\t    edges as Rule 13 build-against edges.'\n15\t- severity: minor\n16\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n17\t  description: BRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004 set gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite\n18\t    while their fallback_approach text says the epic is removed under the other outcome;\n19\t    Rule 3 maps epic-may-not-exist to action=remove.\n20\t- severity: major\n21\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n22\t  description: human_process_gates is scored 0 on E007 and BRANCH-B-E004 with rationales\n23\t    equivalent to E005/E009/E010 (\"no human approval step beyond standard review\"),\n24\t    which are scored 1. Per the rubric (+1 = None) the 0s should be 1; E008 and BRANCH-A-E004\n25\t    (0, hedged rationales) are arguable. Normalize the signal across epics.\n26\t- severity: minor\n27\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n28\t  description: E007 and E010 acceptance criteria assert persisted results and comparison-view\n29\t    outcomes built by E005/E008 without DAG edges to those epics. Defensible as integration-level\n30\t    ACs since both delegate to the execution capability, but the DAG does not order\n31\t    E005/E008 before those ACs can be validated; consider edges or rewording.\n32\t- severity: minor\n33\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n34\t  description: 'BRANCH-B-E004 (greenfield service: execution API, run lifecycle, multi-tenancy,\n35\t    orchestration, provider adapter, deployment) risks exceeding ~2 weeks of work;\n36\t    split at branch instantiation if E001 selects outcome B with a large recommended\n37\t    architecture.'\n38\terror: null\n39\t---\n40\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9002\n41\t\n42\t## Review Summary\n43\t\n44\tScore: 12/14 — pass\n45\tRecommendation: accept\n46\t\n47\tFull decomposition (triage: null, 12 epics including 2 conditional branch epics).\n48\tStructurally sound: DAG diagram and epic frontmatter dependencies match exactly,\n49\tbranch files are correctly configured, HLR coverage is complete, and all\n50\tarchitecture claims verify against `.context/architecture-context/`. Two\n51\tcriteria score 1 for metadata-level defects (incomplete `gated_by` coverage on\n52\tepics with two Investigation dependencies; inconsistent `human_process_gates`\n53\tsignal values across epics with identically-worded rationales). Both fall\n54\twithin those criteria's explicit 1-point bands (\"gating metadata has issues\";\n55\t\"1-2 arguable signal values\"), so they score 1 rather than 0; they are recorded\n56\tas issues for a future revision pass but do not warrant blocking the\n57\tdecomposition.\n58\t\n59\t## Criterion Details\n60\t\n61\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n62\t\n63\tAll nine HLRs map to at least one epic; the traceability matrix in the summary\n64\tis present and accurate against the epic files' HLR Traceability sections.\n65\tAll P0 HLRs (HLR-1 via the branch E004 epics, HLR-2 via E006, HLR-3 via E005)\n66\tare covered by P0 epics. E001–E003 correctly inherit P0 (they gate P0 work).\n67\tE007–E010 are P1, matching their P1 HLRs. E011 (docs-authoring) is P0 via the\n68\tdocs-authoring priority exception (strategy `priority: Critical` → P0), which\n69\tis correctly applied and flagged in Health Warnings. HLR-8 (P2) is bundled\n70\tinto the P0 execution/persistence epics with a valid justification — it shares\n71\tthe run-record data structure and persistence surface with the P0 work, so it\n72\tis coupled scope rather than a deferrable feature (no priority collapse). HLR-9\n73\t(P2) is covered by E011. No orphaned requirements.\n74\t\n75\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n76\t\n77\tNo circular dependencies. Every edge in the summary's Mermaid diagram (drawn\n78\tdependency → dependent, per convention) has a matching `dependencies` entry in\n79\tthe target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa — verified edge by edge for all\n80\t12 epics. No konflux-onboarding / license-validation / repo-onboarding epics\n81\texist, and correctly so: all five affected components have architecture\n82\tcontext files, so no onboarding chain is generated. Branch files\n83\tBRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004 both set `branch` (A/B, matching filename) and\n84\t`gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9002-E001` with E001 in `dependencies`; no main-plan epic\n85\tlists a branch epic in its `dependencies` (E007–E010 correctly depend on the\n86\tInvestigation E001 instead, as required). Rule 11 edges (all implementations →\n87\tE011) are present and correctly do not trigger priority inheritance. Critical\n88\tpath length 4 (E001 → E005 → E008 → E011) is reasonable for a strategy of this\n89\tsize. E005/E006 and E007/E008/E009/E010 parallelism is preserved.\n90\t\n91\tMinor: E007's and E010's acceptance criteria assert outcomes that depend on\n92\tE005's and E008's artifacts (\"results are persisted against the evaluated\n93\tmodel's registered version\"; custom task results \"appear in the same result\n94\tviews/comparisons\") without DAG edges to those epics. This is defensible —\n95\tboth epics delegate to the execution capability rather than building against\n96\tthe registry API directly (unlike E008's comparison view, which correctly has\n97\tan E005 edge), so these read as integration-level ACs — but the DAG does not\n98\tguarantee E005/E008 land before E007/E010's ACs can be validated.\n99\t\n100\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n101\t\n102\tEvery epic has a single component/team tuple, and all team assignments match\n103\tthe owning teams in `.context/architecture-context/` (TrustyAI/TrustyAI,\n104\tModel Registry/Model Registry, Llama Stack/RHOAI GenAI, Dashboard/RHOAI\n105\tDashboard, Data Science Pipelines/RHOAI Pipelines, Documentation/RHOAI\n106\tDocumentation). No epic bundles multiple components or teams. No scope\n107\tduplication across siblings: the branch E004 epics build the execution\n108\tcapability, E006 builds the suite catalog/delivery, E008 is the only UI epic,\n109\tE005 the only persistence epic. No work-product type mixing — E011 is the sole\n110\tcontent epic (docs-authoring); E009's step usage material is incidental\n111\tdocumentation, correctly folded into the code epic.\n112\t\n113\tMinor: BRANCH-B-E004 (greenfield service: execution API, run lifecycle,\n114\tproject-scoped multi-tenancy, lm-eval orchestration, provider adapter surface,\n115\tper-project deployment) is at risk of exceeding ~2 weeks. Acceptable as a\n116\tsingle branch instantiation mirroring branch A's functional envelope, but if\n117\tE001 selects outcome B and the recommended architecture is large, it should be\n118\tsplit at instantiation time.\n119\t\n120\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n121\t\n122\tTypes are correct: E001–E003 are genuine Investigations (each resolves a\n123\tstrategy open question whose answer rewrites downstream epics — engine choice,\n124\tresult schema, licensing), and every Implementation epic produces a concrete\n125\tartifact. All `gated_by` targets appear in the corresponding epic's direct\n126\t`dependencies`. All epics with `gated_by` have `gate_failure_impact` with both\n127\t`action` and `fallback_approach` populated.\n128\t\n129\tTwo gating metadata defects (within this criterion's 1-point band):\n130\t\n131\t- E005 depends on two Investigations (E001, E002) but the scalar `gated_by`\n132\t  records only E002; E006 depends on E001 and E003 but `gated_by` records only\n133\t  E003. The decomposition's own DAG justification labels E001→E005 and E001→E006\n134\t  as rewrite gates (\"approach/scope changes with the outcome\"), so the\n135\t  engine-decision gate on the persistence and suite-delivery epics is not\n136\t  recorded in `gated_by` — automated consumers that detect gates from\n137\t  `gated_by` will miss it. (The schema permits only one `gated_by` value; the\n138\t  summary or justification should at minimum reconcile this, e.g. by\n139\t  justifying E001→E005/E006 as Rule 13 build-against edges rather than gates.)\n140\t- BRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004 set `gate_failure_impact.action: rewrite`,\n141\t  but their `fallback_approach` text says the epic \"is removed\" when the other\n142\t  outcome is selected. Rule 3 maps \"epic may not exist\" to `remove`; the action\n143\t  value contradicts the stated fallback.\n144\t\n145\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n146\t\n147\tCorrect rubric usage throughout: all three Investigation epics carry\n148\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric), all Implementation epics (including\n149\tboth branch epics and the docs-authoring epic) carry `ai_signals` (9-signal\n150\trubric), and every epic has an ai-signals rationale file whose values match the\n151\tfrontmatter. Investigation signal values are well-justified against the rubric\n152\t(e.g. E001 `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` for peripheral cluster\n153\tvalidation; E003 `human_judgment_required: -1` for peripheral legal review).\n154\tComputed `ai_implementability` fields were not checked, per instructions.\n155\t\n156\tOne inconsistency (conservative-direction, does not change any routing\n157\toutcome): `human_process_gates` is scored 0 on E007 and BRANCH-B-E004 with\n158\trationales (\"No human approval step beyond standard review\" / \"No stated human\n159\tapproval gate; standard review applies\") that are treated as +1 (\"None\") on\n160\tE005, E009, and E010 with identically-worded rationales. Per the rubric's +1\n161\tcondition (\"None\"), E007 and BRANCH-B-E004 should be +1. E008 and\n162\tBRANCH-A-E004 (0, with hedged rationales mentioning possible UX sign-off /\n163\tTech Preview release scrutiny) are arguable but defensible.\n164\t\n165\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n166\t\n167\tEvery epic has concrete, testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated\n168\tACs are present wherever applicable: BRANCH-A-E004 has a rollback/non-regression\n169\tAC for the extended Tech Preview path; E008 has a feature-flag AC (off by\n170\tdefault, via the Dashboard's standard per-component mechanism); E011\n171\t(docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC; E005 has a\n172\tregistry-health AC; implementation epics carry build-pipeline-green ACs. No\n173\tepic body references sibling epics by draft ID — verified by grep; dependency\n174\trelationships live only in frontmatter, and bodies describe dependencies in\n175\tplain terms (\"the result schema prototype\", \"the evaluation execution\n176\tcapability\").\n177\t\n178\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n179\t\n180\tAll strategy scope is covered: all nine HLRs, all three open questions\n181\tdispositioned to Investigation epics E001/E002/E003 with bounded outcomes\n182\t(two branches) for the engine question, out-of-scope commitments respected\n183\t(E009 explicitly keeps humans in the promotion decision; no arena-style or\n184\tfine-tuning feedback work appears anywhere), and strategy-level context\n185\tcarried forward (Tech Preview graduation risk → BRANCH-A-E004 scope and Health\n186\tWarnings; disconnected-environment licensing risk → E003/E006 and E011 scope).\n187\tCross-epic consistency holds: both evaluation targets (KServe endpoint, Llama\n188\tStack distribution) named in HLR-1 appear in the branch epics' scope and ACs;\n189\tthe schema work in E002 covers scores, suite versions, and cost/duration\n190\tmetadata, all of which E005 persists. Documentation support is covered by the\n191\tE011 docs-authoring epic (Rule 11). Missing strategy sections (Impacted Teams,\n192\tACs, NFRs) are explicitly flagged as health warnings rather than silently\n193\tdropped.\n194\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a64c0b42b7b2eae3","agent_id":"a960b39ae8f09b974","session_id":"811fcb9e-f25c-43e8-a4d0-4711eb32341e","uuid":"08800798-a840-4a3d-9d5a-c09cb52c782f","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:57:23.966Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-89eccbb91b9aa244","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
