isted, version-linked results produced by the persistence work. |\n101\t| E005 → E011 | Rule 11 | Docs are blocked by all Implementation epics; always last. |\n102\t| E006 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n103\t| E007 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n104\t| E008 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n105\t| E009 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n106\t| E010 → E011 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n107\t\n108\tDeliberately absent edges:\n109\t\n110\t- No `konflux-onboarding` edges: every affected component is already Konflux\n111\t  onboarded per architecture context, so no onboarding chain is generated\n112\t  (Rules 19–20 do not fire — no new component enters the platform).\n113\t- E007/E008/E009/E010 do **not** depend on the branch E004 epics: branch epics\n114\t  are not main-plan nodes, and these epics exist under both outcomes. They\n115\t  depend on the outcome of the Investigation (E001) instead.\n116\t- E005 and E006 are parallel (different components/teams, no shared artifact).\n117\t- E007, E008, E009, E010 are mutually parallel (different components/teams).\n118\t\n119\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n120\t\n121\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n122\t|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n123\t| HLR-1 | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 | Evaluation launch against served models (KServe endpoint or Llama Stack distribution) — exactly one branch epic is instantiated. |\n124\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E006 | Standard suites (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag via lm-eval task names) out of the box. |\n125\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E005 | Results persisted and linked to the Model Registry version. |\n126\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E010 | Custom evaluation tasks (own datasets/metrics) through the same flow. |\n127\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E008 | Dashboard runs/status/scores and side-by-side version comparison. |\n128\t| HLR-6 | P1 | E009 | Evaluation as a Data Science Pipelines step for promotion gating. |\n129\t| HLR-7 | P1 | E007 | Llama Stack eval provider API backed by the platform service. |\n130\t| HLR-8 | P2 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004, E005 | Cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) is recorded by the execution capability and persisted with results. Bundled, not split: it shares the run-record data structure and persistence surface with the P0 work — splitting would duplicate the same schema (Step 4 priority-split test fails). |\n131\t| HLR-9 | P2 | E011 | End-to-end product documentation including custom task authoring. |\n132\t\n133\tAll nine HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n134\t\n135\t## Open Question Disposition\n136\t\n137\t| Open question | Disposition | Rationale |\n138\t|---------------|-------------|-----------|\n139\t| 1. Execution engine (LMEvalJob vs new service) | Investigation epic E001 with conditional branches | The strategy explicitly requests a technical investigation with a recommendation; the answer changes which execution epic exists and what every integration epic builds against. Two bounded outcomes → branch epics for the E004 slot. |\n140\t| 2. Result schema for Model Registry custom properties | Investigation epic E002 | Strategy requires a prototype against real MMLU output before committing; the answer rewrites the persistence epic (E005). Unbounded schema design → gate, no branches. |\n141\t| 3. Benchmark dataset licensing / redistribution | Investigation epic E003 | The answer rewrites the out-of-the-box suite delivery epic (E006): bundled redistribution vs customer-fetch mechanism. |\n142\t\n143\t## Conditional Branches\n144\t\n145\tThe execution-engine investigation (E001) has exactly two bounded outcomes:\n146\t\n147\t- **Branch A** — build on TrustyAI's LMEvalJob (Tech Preview) as the single\n148\t  execution path: `RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-A-E004.md`.\n149\t- **Branch B** — a new evaluation service that also serves Llama Stack's eval\n150\t  provider API: `RHAISTRAT-9002-BRANCH-B-E004.md`.\n151\t\n152\tBoth branch files set `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9002-E001`, include E001 in\n153\t`dependencies`, and carry `gate_failure_impact`. No main-plan epic lists a\n154\tbranch epic in its `dependencies`; epics that depend on the execution\n155\tcapability depend on E001 (the Investigation) instead. Branch epics are\n156\texcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n157\t\n158\t## Component Mapping Notes\n159\t\n160\t- All strategy components map 1:1 to canonical names in\n161\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt` (TrustyAI, Llama Stack, Dashboard, Data\n162\t  Science Pipelines, Model Registry).\n163\t- All five have architecture context files → all are in the platform; no\n164\t  repo-onboarding / konflux-onboarding / license-validation chain is generated.\n165\t- BRANCH-B-E004 (a new evaluation service) has no canonical component of its\n166\t  own; it is filed under the closest parent-level component **TrustyAI** (the\n167\t  platform's existing model-quality/evaluation component). If branch B is\n168\t  selected, a component rename/registration may be requested at execution time.\n169\t- Documentation is a canonical component but has no architecture context file;\n170\t  it is a content (not code) component, handled as `docs-authoring` with no\n171\t  provisioning chain.\n172\t- **Model Registry is treated as active**, not passive: while custom properties\n173\t  already exist as the extension point, the strategy's linkage requirement\n174\t  (results tied to the *deployed* model version) builds on the registry's\n175\t  role as source of truth linking model versions to serving deployments, so\n176\t  E002/E005 are owned by the Model Registry team.\n177\t\n178\t## Health Warnings\n179\t\n180\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n181\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n182\t  no Non-Functional Requirements, no Technical Approach, and no Staff Engineer\n183\t  Input. Teams were derived from architecture-context owning teams; the\n184\t  Documentation epic's team (\"RHOAI Documentation\") is assumed since no owner\n185\t  is stated anywhere. Acceptance criteria below are derived from HLRs.\n186\t- **Docs priority inversion (rule-driven)**: HLR-9 is P2, but the docs-authoring\n187\t  priority exception derives the epic's priority from the strategy-level\n188\t  `priority: Critical` → P0. Flagged so a human can confirm this is intended.\n189\t- **Scope trap — disconnected environments**: HLR-2 promises out-of-the-box\n190\t  suites, but redistribution in disconnected environments is license-gated\n191\t  (E003). If most suites turn out to be customer-fetch, HLR-2's \"out of the\n192\t  box\" promise degrades; the E003 gate exists to surface this before\n193\t  implementation.\n194\t- **Tech Preview foundation**: Branch A builds on the LMEvalJob integration,\n195\t  which is Tech Preview; graduation risk is inherent to that outcome.\n196\t\n197\t## Tiered Delivery\n198\t\n199\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing: all\n200\tcomponents are in-platform, and the upstream dependencies (lm-evaluation-harness,\n201\tLlama Stack) are already integrated. The conditional branches above handle the\n202\tstrategy's internal uncertainty instead.\n203\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a64c0b42b7b2eae3","agent_id":"a960b39ae8f09b974","session_id":"811fcb9e-f25c-43e8-a4d0-4711eb32341e","uuid":"08d43f83-b8aa-4b12-a4f9-ddff0898b12a","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:57:46.426Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1b5aca1bb2db903","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
