rom inheritance and\nexplicitly flagged for human confirmation in Health Warnings.\n\nHLR-8 (P2, cost/duration metadata) is bundled into the branch epics and E005\nrather than split. This is correct, not priority collapse: the metadata shares\nthe run-record data structure and the Model Registry persistence surface with\nthe P0 scores, so it fails the \"distinct and deferrable\" test (independent\nuser value AND no shared data structures) — splitting would duplicate the\nsame schema. The justification is stated in the traceability matrix.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\n- **Diagram/frontmatter cross-check**: all 17 edges in the Mermaid diagram\n  (`graph TD`, dependency → dependent) have matching `dependencies` entries\n  in the target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa. No dangling references,\n  no arrows drawn in the reverse direction.\n- **No circular dependencies.** Critical path E001 → E005 → E008 → E011\n  (length 4) is reasonable; the extensive parallelism (E005 ∥ E006;\n  E007/E008/E009/E010 mutually parallel) is Rule 14-justified — different\n  repos/teams, no shared build-against artifacts.\n- **Branch compliance**: BRANCH-A-E004 and BRANCH-B-E004 both set `branch`\n  (A/B matching filename), `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001`, include E001 in\n  `dependencies`, and carry populated `gate_failure_impact` (action: remove,\n  with fallback). No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in `dependencies`;\n  epics depending on the execution capability correctly depend on / are\n  gated by the Investigation E001 instead.\n- **Implementation-type parallelism**: no `konflux-onboarding`,\n  `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10\n  cannot be violated. This is correct: all five code components have\n  architecture context files marked \"Konflux onboarded: yes\", so Rule 20\n  does not fire; BRANCH-B's new service is filed under the closest parent\n  component (TrustyAI) with the mapping logged, per the component-name\n  constraint.\n- **Edg